1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Hiring an outside red team is easy.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Building one your own organization owns — that's

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,000
the real advantage.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Here's how to stand one up.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,652
First, what a red team actually is.

6
00:00:15,652 --> 00:00:19,826
Borrowed from military and intelligence practice and adapted

7
00:00:19,826 --> 00:00:24,000
for business, it's structured contrarian analysis: a group

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,174
whose job is to attack a plan on

9
00:00:28,174 --> 00:00:32,348
purpose, so its blind spots show up in

10
00:00:32,348 --> 00:00:36,000
a meeting instead of in the market.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,240
Step one: the charter.

12
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:42,720
Write down what the red team exists to

13
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:45,520
do and what it doesn't.

14
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:50,000
It is not the department of no, and

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,920
it is not there to grade people.

16
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:58,400
Its single job: make every major decision survive

17
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,880
a serious, structured challenge before the company commits

18
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:04,000
to it.

19
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,797
Step two, and this is the one most

20
00:01:07,797 --> 00:01:09,695
companies get wrong: independence.

21
00:01:09,695 --> 00:01:13,492
If the red team reports to the executive

22
00:01:13,492 --> 00:01:17,288
who owns the plan, it will pull its

23
00:01:17,288 --> 00:01:21,085
punches and tell that person what they want

24
00:01:21,085 --> 00:01:22,034
to hear.

25
00:01:22,034 --> 00:01:25,831
Give it a separate reporting line and explicit

26
00:01:25,831 --> 00:01:28,678
air cover to deliver bad news.

27
00:01:28,678 --> 00:01:32,000
Independence is what makes it worth having.

28
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Step three: cadence and method, so it's a

29
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,500
capability and not a stunt.

30
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:42,500
Pick a rhythm — every major bet, or

31
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:46,000
every quarter — and a repeatable method.

32
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Run a key assumptions check: rewrite the plan

33
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
as falsifiable beliefs and score each by confidence

34
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
and consequence.

35
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,500
Then a premortem.

36
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:00,500
Same moves, every time, so the team gets

37
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,000
sharper with reps.

38
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:07,333
Start small: one charter, one independent owner, one

39
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:09,333
decision next quarter.

40
00:02:09,333 --> 00:02:14,667
Comment the decision you'd point your first red

41
00:02:14,667 --> 00:02:20,000
team at, and I'll send the starter charter.
